WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.427
We will reuse one small order dataset throughout many of the examples in this module.

00:00:05.427 --> 00:00:11.312
Keeping the dataset simple makes it easier to see what each Pandas operation is doing.

00:00:11.312 --> 00:00:13.732
Each row represents an order.

00:00:13.732 --> 00:00:17.087
The order ID identifies the transaction.

00:00:17.087 --> 00:00:19.997
The date tells us when the order occurred.

00:00:19.997 --> 00:00:27.022
Region, channel, and category give us different ways to group the data, and amount is the numeric value that we will

00:00:27.022 --> 00:00:28.793
summarize.

00:00:29.331 --> 00:00:33.718
This is a useful structure because it resembles many real datasets.

00:00:33.718 --> 00:00:38.853
In a business setting, the rows could represent purchases.

00:00:38.853 --> 00:00:41.983
In healthcare, the rows might represent visits or claims.

00:00:41.983 --> 00:00:47.808
In education, they could represent course registrations or student activities.

00:00:47.808 --> 00:00:53.633
The Pandas techniques are the same even when the subject of the data changes.

00:00:53.633 --> 00:00:58.308
As we continue, watch how the shape of this table changes.

00:00:58.308 --> 00:01:03.068
Sometimes we will keep the same number of rows and add information.

00:01:03.068 --> 00:01:09.078
Other times we will reduce the table into summaries or reshape it into a different format.
